  "narrative": "The four-background controls strongly support the robustness of the accepted tumor-suppressor-loss contrasts within the supplied dataset. SMARCA4 loss after excluding TP53-, KEAP1-, and PTEN-loss cases remains associated with much stronger dependency_composite: R27 gives +0.816 [0.771, 0.860] in the accepted negative direction. I accept H60 and close this specific SMARCA4 co-mutation robustness test. KEAP1 loss after excluding SMARCA4-, TP53-, and PTEN-loss cases remains associated with higher composite values/weaker dependency: R28 gives +0.809 [0.768, 0.850]. I accept H61 and close this specific KEAP1 robustness test, consistent with the already validated broad KEAP1 evidence and triple-background H34. TP53 loss after excluding SMARCA4-, KEAP1-, and PTEN-loss cases remains associated with higher composite values/weaker dependency: R29 gives +0.395 [0.291, 0.499]. I accept H62 and keep the TP53 investigation active because it has now shown consistent broad, triple-background, and four-background effects, although an independent validation interval for TP53 is still not yet available.\n\nThe PTEN triple-background control is the central new evidence. PTEN loss versus no PTEN loss in cells without SMARCA4, TP53, or KEAP1 loss gives +0.252 [0.169, 0.335] in the accepted stronger-dependency direction (lower composite values). The lower confidence bound is above the 0.15 threshold, so H53 is accepted as a dataset-level independent background association pending validation. I request independent validation of H53 because PTEN was initially accepted from a broad comparison whose lower bound was slightly below the threshold, and because this comparison is the cleanest test of whether PTEN-loss association is not merely due to co-occurrence with the other accepted predictors. If validation confirms the direction and meaningful magnitude, PTEN becomes a validated moderate independent association. If validation attenuates, I would retain the broad H52 cautiously but downgrade confidence in a fully independent PTEN association.\n\nThe PTEN-background interaction tests do not support context dependence. H64 gives a small contrary-to-claim signed interaction for SMARCA4-loss (-0.107 [-0.321, 0.108]); H65 gives a near-null positive-direction KEAP1-loss interaction (-0.145 [-0.354, 0.064]); H66 is nominally at threshold for TP53-loss but extremely imprecise and includes null and both signs (+0.157 [-0.145, 0.459]). These are reject and closed. Given multiple interaction testing and smaller subgroup cells, the intervals do not provide a credible basis for context-specific claims, although they do not absolutely rule out modest interactions.\n\nThe new lung-associated drivers provide no evidence in their pre-registered directions: ERBB2 amplification R34 is near null (+0.018 [-0.094, 0.131]), BRAF V600E R35 is small and imprecise (+0.049 [-0.071, 0.169]), PIK3CA mutation R36 is null (-0.013 [-0.092, 0.067]), and NF1 loss R37 is null in the higher-composite direction (-0.002 [-0.103, 0.098]). I reject and close these. The Avana-library technical screen H74 is also null/small (+0.052 [-0.038, 0.143]), so there is no main-effect evidence that library protocol is the driver of the accepted genetic associations. This is reassuring, but it does not exclude library- or batch-specific interactions; existing library and culture robustness claims remain for possible follow-up. Overall, the new evidence expands the robustness of SMARCA4, KEAP1, TP53, and PTEN associations while reducing support for PTEN-background interaction and for several common driver/technical main-effect explanations. All conclusions remain associative within the supplied dataset.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 3 appraise results. Newly accepted robustness claims: H60 SMARCA4 four-background +0.816 [0.771,0.860] (closed); H61 KEAP1 four-background +0.809 [0.768,0.850] (closed); H62 TP53 four-background +0.395 [0.291,0.499] (active, unvalidated); H53 PTEN triple-background excluding SMARCA4/TP53/KEAP1 +0.252 [0.169,0.335] (active, validation requested). Rejected/closed: PTEN-background interactions H64-H66; new drivers ERBB2 H45, BRAF H46, PIK3CA H47, NF1 H50; technical Avana library main effect H74. Current accepted predictors: SMARCA4 loss lower composite/stronger dependency (broad validated H5; H19/H60 robust); KEAP1 loss higher composite/weaker dependency (broad H14; triple H34 validated; H61 robust); TP53 loss higher composite/weaker dependency (broad H13; triple H35; four-background H62; validation pending or future); PTEN loss lower composite/stronger dependency (broad H52 accepted moderate; triple H53 accepted pending validation). No credible evidence for marker assays, marker-by-signature interactions, ARID1A, STK11, MYC amp, MTAP, KRAS, EGFR amp, RB1 tested direction, CDKN2A, ERBB2, BRAF, PIK3CA, NF1, APC/SMAD4/MSI as not yet run, or Avana library main effect as meaningful. Interactions among accepted predictors are generally null or imprecise. Next decisions: await H53 validation. If H53 validates, close PTEN robustness and keep PTEN as moderate validated independent association; if attenuates, keep broad H52 cautiously but downgrade independence. Consider scheduled or next validation for TP53 H35/H62 if PTEN becomes resolved and validation remains useful. Remaining analyses should prioritize context/technical robustness for PTEN and TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1: H15/H16/H23 for SMARCA4 subtype/culture/library, H30/H31/H36/H37 and batch/library robustness for KEAP1/TP53, H54-H56/H63/H70/H79 for PTEN subtype/culture/library/interaction, and untested APC/SMAD4/MSI/matched-omics technical screens H71-H73/H75. Keep all claims associational; no causality or external generalization."
